                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Assuming the question is asking how well people want their sheep meat cooked, it is important to note that different cuts of sheep meat require different levels of cooking. For example, a leg of lamb may be cooked to medium-rare or medium, while ground lamb should be cooked to well-done to ensure food safety.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What are the different cuts of sheep meat?\n2. What are the recommended cooking temperatures for each cut?\n3. How can you tell when sheep meat is cooked to the desired level?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Different cuts of sheep meat include leg, shoulder, rack, loin, and ground lamb.\n2. Recommended cooking temperatures vary depending on the cut of meat. For example, a leg of lamb should be cooked to an internal temperature of 145\u00b0F for medium-rare or 160\u00b0F for medium, while ground lamb should be cooked to an internal temperature of 160\u00b0F for well-done.\n3. To tell when sheep meat is cooked to the desired level, use a meat thermometer to check the internal temperature. Additionally, the color and texture of the meat can also indicate doneness. For example, medium-rare lamb should be pink in the center and slightly firm to the touch, while well-done lamb will be brown throughout and very firm.",
