                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient is a 61-year-old man who underwent an emergency cardiac catherization with stenting for unstable angina pectoris two weeks ago. He now presents with decreased urinary output, malaise, and mottled, reticulated purplish discoloration of the feet. His laboratory studies show leukocytosis, elevated erythrocyte sedimentation rate, and elevated serum creatinine. Renal biopsy shows intravascular spindle-shaped vacuoles. \n\nThe most likely cause of this patient's symptoms is cholesterol embolization. \n\nCholesterol embolization syndrome (CES) is a systemic disease caused by the release of cholesterol crystals from atherosclerotic plaques into the bloodstream, leading to the occlusion of small blood vessels in various organs, including the kidneys. The classic triad of CES includes livedo reticularis, acute renal failure, and eosinophilia. The patient in this case has two of these features: livedo reticularis and acute renal failure. The laboratory studies also show eosinophilia, which is a common finding in CES. The intravascular spindle-shaped vacuoles seen on renal biopsy are also consistent with CES. \n\nRenal papillary necrosis is a condition characterized by ischemic necrosis of the renal papillae, which can be caused by various factors, including diabetes mellitus and the use of nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) such as naproxen. However, this condition typically presents with gross hematuria and flank pain, which are not present in this patient. \n\nAllergic interstitial nephritis is an immune-mediated inflammatory condition of the renal interstitium, which can be caused by various drugs, including NSAIDs. However, this condition typically presents with fever, rash, and eosinophilia, which are not present in this patient. \n\nEosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis (formerly known as Churg-Strauss syndrome) is a rare autoimmune disease characterized by eosinophilic infiltration of various organs, including the kidneys. However, this condition typically presents with asthma, sinusitis, and peripheral neuropathy, which are not present in this patient. \n\nPolyarteritis nodosa is a systemic vasculitis that affects medium-sized arteries, including the renal arteries. However, this condition typically presents with fever, weight loss, and multiple organ involvement, which are not present in this patient. \n\nTherefore, the most likely cause of this patient's symptoms is cholesterol embol",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "f7def9be-0698-40a0-90eb-4a10a71c2526",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "C) Cholesterol embolization.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "C",
