                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what unstable angina pectoris is?\n\nProfessor: Unstable angina pectoris is a type of chest pain that occurs when the heart muscle doesn't get enough blood flow and oxygen. It is considered unstable because it can happen even when a person is at rest and can be a sign of an impending heart attack.\n\nStudent: What is a cardiac catherization with stenting?\n\nProfessor: Cardiac catherization is a medical procedure that involves inserting a thin tube called a catheter into a blood vessel in the arm or leg and threading it up to the heart. Stenting is a procedure where a small metal mesh tube is placed in the narrowed or blocked artery to help keep it open and improve blood flow to the heart.\n\nStudent: What is the significance of the patient's decreased urinary output and malaise?\n\nProfessor: Decreased urinary output and malaise can be signs of kidney dysfunction or failure. This is a serious complication that can occur after a cardiac catherization with stenting.\n\nStudent: What is the significance of the patient's mottled, reticulated purplish discoloration of the feet?\n\nProfessor: Mottled, reticulated purplish discoloration of the feet can be a sign of cholesterol embolization, which is a complication that can occur after a cardiac catherization with stenting. It happens when small pieces of cholesterol break off from the plaque in the arteries and travel to other parts of the body, including the feet.\n\nStudent: Can you explain what intravascular spindle-shaped vacuoles are?\n\nProfessor: Intravascular spindle-shaped vacuoles are abnormal structures that can be seen in a renal biopsy. They are often associated with cholesterol embolization.\n\nStudent: Based on the patient's symptoms and laboratory studies, what is the most likely cause of his condition?\n\nProfessor: The most likely cause of the patient's symptoms is cholesterol embolization. This is supported by the mottled, reticulated purplish discoloration of the feet, the intravascular spindle-shaped vacuoles seen on renal biopsy, and the elevated erythrocyte sedimentation rate.",
