                        "cot_trigger_template": "",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Let's differentiate using step by step reasoning like a medical expert: \n\nThe patient has decreased urinary output and malaise. This could be caused by renal papillary necrosis, cholesterol embolization, or polyarteritis nodosa.\n\nThe patient has type 2 diabetes mellitus and osteoarthritis of the hips. This could be caused by eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis.\n\nThe patient was started on aspirin, clopidogrel, and metoprolol after the coronary intervention. This could be caused by cholesterol embolization.\n\nThe patient's temperature is 38\u00b0C (100.4\u00b0F), pulse is 93/min, and blood pressure is 125/85 mm Hg. Examination shows mottled, reticulated purplish discoloration of the feet. This could be caused by cholesterol embolization.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin count 14 g/dL\nLeukocyte count 16,400/mm3\nSegmented neutrophils 56%\nEosinophils 11%\nLymphocytes 31%\nMonocytes 2%\nPlatelet count 260,000/mm3\nErythrocyte sedimentation rate 68 mm/h\nSerum\nUrea nitrogen 25 mg/dL\nCreatinine 4.2 mg/dL\n\nThis could be caused by renal papillary necrosis, cholesterol embolization, or polyarteritis nodosa.\n\nRenal biopsy shows intravascular spindle-shaped vacuoles. This could be caused by eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis.\n\nBased on the above information, the most likely cause of the patient's symptoms is cholesterol embolization.",
