                    {
                        "id": "846ce7a9-e513-43f7-a9da-1c2c5f56495e",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "D) Increased cerebrospinal fluid protein with normal cell count\n\nThis patient is presenting with symptoms consistent with acute gastroenteritis, including vomiting, diarrhea, and bloody stools. The presence of guaiac-positive stool suggests gastrointestinal bleeding. The patient also has a fever, tachycardia, and hypertension, which may be indicative of dehydration and/or sepsis. On physical exam, the patient has diffuse abdominal tenderness without rebound or guarding, which is consistent with gastroenteritis. However, the development of lower extremity weakness and tingling on the second day of illness is concerning for a neurologic complication.\n\nThe most likely diagnosis in this case is Guillain-Barr\u00e9 syndrome (GBS), a rare but serious autoimmune disorder that affects the peripheral nervous system. GBS is often preceded by a viral or bacterial infection, and symptoms typically begin with weakness and tingling in the legs that spreads to the arms and upper body. In severe cases, GBS can cause paralysis and respiratory failure.\n\nThe lab finding that is most likely to be seen in this patient is an increased cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) protein level with a normal cell count. This is a characteristic finding in GBS, as the disorder is associated with an inflammatory response that leads to increased protein levels in the CSF. Other CSF findings that may be seen in GBS include xanthochromia (yellow discoloration due to breakdown of red blood cells) and oligoclonal bands (abnormal bands of immunoglobulins in the CSF). However, these findings are less specific for GBS and may also be seen in other neurologic disorders.\n\nGram stain positive CSF is not typically seen in GBS, as the disorder is not caused by a bacterial infection. Peripheral eosinophilia is also not a typical finding in GBS, although it may be seen in some cases of parasitic infections or drug reactions.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "f38146a1-4d3a-4d2d-a7e8-c0217a3cb44e",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
