                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient presents with symptoms of gastroenteritis, including vomiting, diarrhea, and bloody stools. He also has a fever, tachycardia, and hypertension, which may indicate dehydration and/or sepsis. On physical exam, he has diffuse abdominal tenderness without rebound or guarding, and stool is guaiac positive, suggesting gastrointestinal bleeding. \n\nThe next day, he develops lower extremity weakness and tingling, which may indicate neurological involvement. On repeat exam, he has diminished patellar deep tendon reflexes and lower extremity weakness. \n\nGiven these findings, the most likely lab finding would be D) Increased cerebrospinal fluid protein with normal cell count. This may suggest a peripheral neuropathy, which can occur as a complication of severe dehydration and electrolyte imbalances. Gram stain positive CSF, peripheral eosinophilia, xanthochromia on cerebrospinal fluid analysis, and oligoclonal bands on cerebrospinal fluid analysis are less likely to be seen in this patient.",
