                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step-by-step procedure:\n1. Identify the patient's symptoms and physical exam findings.\n2. Consider potential diagnoses based on the symptoms and physical exam findings.\n3. Determine which potential diagnosis is most likely based on the information given.\n4. Identify the lab finding that is most commonly associated with the most likely diagnosis.\n\nGoing through each step:\n1. The patient has been experiencing vomiting, loose stools (including bloody stools), decreased oral intake, and lower extremity weakness and tingling. On physical exam, he has a fever, high blood pressure, tachycardia, and diffuse abdominal tenderness without rebound or guarding. He also has dry mucous membranes and hyperactive bowel sounds.\n2. Potential diagnoses based on these symptoms and physical exam findings include gastroenteritis, inflammatory bowel disease, sepsis, and Guillain-Barr\u00e9 syndrome.\n3. Given the patient's symptoms and physical exam findings, Guillain-Barr\u00e9 syndrome is the most likely diagnosis. This is supported by the presence of lower extremity weakness and tingling, which are classic symptoms of Guillain-Barr\u00e9 syndrome. The negative ultrasound for appendicitis rules out that potential diagnosis, and the diffuse abdominal tenderness without rebound or guarding is not specific to any one diagnosis.\n4. The lab finding most commonly associated with Guillain-Barr\u00e9 syndrome is increased cerebrospinal fluid protein with normal cell count (option D). This is due to the immune system attacking the myelin sheath around nerves, leading to inflammation and increased protein in the cerebrospinal fluid. Gram stain positive CSF (option A) and xanthochromia on cerebrospinal fluid analysis (option C) are more commonly associated with subarachnoid hemorrhage. Peripheral eosinophilia (option B) is more commonly associated with parasitic infections or allergic reactions. Oligoclonal bands on cerebrospinal fluid analysis (option E) are more commonly associated with multiple sclerosis.",
