                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step 1: Let's work out every answer option and consider if it is the correct option.\nA) Gram stain positive CSF - This is unlikely as there is no indication of meningitis in the patient's symptoms or physical exam.\nB) Peripheral eosinophilia - This is also unlikely as there is no indication of an allergic or parasitic reaction in the patient's symptoms or physical exam.\nC) Xanthochromia on cerebrospinal fluid analysis - This is unlikely as there is no indication of bleeding in the brain or spinal cord in the patient's symptoms or physical exam.\nD) Increased cerebrospinal fluid protein with normal cell count - This is a possibility as the patient is experiencing lower extremity weakness and tingling, which could indicate nerve damage. An increased protein level in the cerebrospinal fluid could be a sign of nerve damage.\nE) Oligoclonal bands on cerebrospinal fluid analysis - This is unlikely as there is no indication of multiple sclerosis in the patient's symptoms or physical exam.\n\nStep 2: Combining all the information gathered, the most likely lab finding in this patient would be an increased cerebrospinal fluid protein with normal cell count. The patient's lower extremity weakness and tingling could indicate nerve damage, and an increased protein level in the cerebrospinal fluid could be a sign of nerve damage.\n\nStep 3: The answer is D) Increased cerebrospinal fluid protein with normal cell count.",
