                    {
                        "id": "8e32f263-0b40-4973-8645-e552fbcc9c96",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-12",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Based on the patient's symptoms and physical exam findings, it is likely that he has a gastrointestinal infection, possibly with bloody diarrhea. The presence of lower extremity weakness and tingling on the second day of presentation suggests that he may be developing a neurological complication, such as Guillain-Barre syndrome (GBS).\n\nGBS is an autoimmune disorder that can occur after a viral or bacterial infection, in which the immune system attacks the peripheral nerves. This can lead to muscle weakness, tingling, and loss of reflexes. In order to confirm a diagnosis of GBS, a lumbar puncture may be performed to analyze the cerebrospinal fluid (CSF).\n\nOf the lab findings listed, the most likely to be seen in this patient with GBS would be D) Increased cerebrospinal fluid protein with normal cell count. This is a characteristic finding in GBS, as the immune system produces antibodies that attack the myelin sheath around the nerves, leading to an increase in protein in the CSF. However, the cell count in the CSF is typically normal, as the immune cells are attacking the peripheral nerves rather than infiltrating the CSF.\n\nA) Gram stain positive CSF would not be expected in GBS, as it is not caused by a bacterial infection.\n\nB) Peripheral eosinophilia is not typically seen in GBS, as it is a disorder of the peripheral nerves rather than the immune system.\n\nC) Xanthochromia on cerebrospinal fluid analysis is a finding that may be seen in subarachnoid hemorrhage, but is not specific to GBS.\n\nE) Oligoclonal bands on cerebrospinal fluid analysis may be seen in multiple sclerosis, but are not typically seen in GBS.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "92263cbc-acd4-4b93-bc0e-e66540880079",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "D) Increased cerebrospinal fluid protein with normal cell count.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "D",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
