                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: The patient is a 4-year-old boy with a recent history of vomiting and diarrhea, bloody stools, decreased oral intake, and diffuse abdominal tenderness. He has a fever, tachycardia, and hypertension. He has received acetaminophen and fluids, but now complains of lower extremity weakness and tingling. On exam, he has diminished patellar deep tendon reflexes and lower extremity weakness.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the most likely diagnosis for this patient?\n2. What is the significance of the patient's hypertension?\n3. What is the significance of the patient's guaiac positive stool?\n4. What is the significance of the patient's lower extremity weakness and tingling?\n5. What lab findings would be most likely seen in this patient?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The most likely diagnosis for this patient is hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS), which is a rare but serious complication of certain types of bacterial infections, particularly those caused by E. coli O157:H7. HUS is characterized by the triad of microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and acute kidney injury.\n2. The patient's hypertension is likely due to the activation of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS) in response to decreased renal perfusion and acute kidney injury.\n3. The patient's guaiac positive stool is indicative of gastrointestinal bleeding, which is a common feature of HUS.\n4. The patient's lower extremity weakness and tingling are likely due to peripheral neuropathy, which is a known complication of HUS.\n5. The lab finding that would be most likely seen in this patient is D) Increased cerebrospinal fluid protein with normal cell count. This is because HUS can cause damage to the blood-brain barrier, leading to increased protein in the cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) without an increase in white blood cells. Gram stain positive CSF (A) would be more indicative of bacterial meningitis, which is not likely in this patient. Peripheral eosinophilia (B) and oligoclonal bands on CSF analysis (E) are not typically seen in HUS. Xanthochromia on CSF analysis (C) is more commonly seen in subarachnoid hemorrhage.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "903a2e66-ebf0-4f74-b50f-730e224c1fd5",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "D) Increased cerebrospinal fluid protein with normal cell count.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "D",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
