                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what the symptoms of the patient are?\n\nProfessor: The patient has been experiencing vomiting and loose stools for 4 days, with bloody stools and decreased oral intake over the last 24 hours. He also has a fever, high blood pressure, and a fast heart rate. On physical exam, he has diffuse tenderness in his abdomen and dry mucous membranes. He also has a positive stool guaiac test.\n\nStudent: What treatment did the patient receive?\n\nProfessor: The patient received 15mg/kg of acetaminophen and fluids were started.\n\nStudent: What happened to the patient the next day?\n\nProfessor: The patient complained of lower extremity weakness and tingling.\n\nStudent: What did the repeat exam show?\n\nProfessor: The repeat exam showed lower extremity weakness with diminished patellar deep tendon reflexes.\n\nStudent: What lab findings would most likely be seen in this patient?\n\nProfessor: The lab finding that would most likely be seen in this patient is D) Increased cerebrospinal fluid protein with normal cell count. This is because the patient is showing signs of Guillain-Barre syndrome, which is a rare autoimmune disorder that affects the peripheral nervous system. In Guillain-Barre syndrome, the immune system attacks the nerves, causing muscle weakness and tingling sensations. An increased cerebrospinal fluid protein with normal cell count is a common finding in this condition. The other options are not associated with Guillain-Barre syndrome.",
