                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nD) Increased cerebrospinal fluid protein with normal cell count\n\nThe patient's symptoms and physical exam findings suggest a possible diagnosis of Guillain-Barre syndrome, which is a rare but serious autoimmune disorder that affects the peripheral nervous system. The lower extremity weakness and tingling, as well as the diminished patellar deep tendon reflexes, are consistent with this diagnosis. The most common laboratory finding in patients with Guillain-Barre syndrome is an increased cerebrospinal fluid protein level with a normal cell count. This is due to the breakdown of the blood-nerve barrier, which allows protein to leak into the cerebrospinal fluid. Gram stain positive CSF, peripheral eosinophilia, xanthochromia on cerebrospinal fluid analysis, and oligoclonal bands on cerebrospinal fluid analysis are not typically associated with Guillain-Barre syndrome.",
