                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Let's use step by step inductive reasoning, given the medical nature of the question. \n\nThe patient is a 3-week-old male newborn who was born at 38 weeks gestation. He has poor weight gain and is currently latching on and breastfeeding well. His mother has a history of Graves' disease and underwent a near-total thyroidectomy in the second trimester of her pregnancy. The patient's temperature is 38.9\u00b0C (102\u00b0F), pulse is 176/min, and respirations are 42/min. He appears irritable and has a paucity of subcutaneous fat. There is swelling of the neck at the midline.\n\nThe most likely cause of the patient's symptoms is transplacental passage of thyroid peroxidase antibodies. This is because the patient's mother has a history of Graves' disease, which is an autoimmune disorder that results in the production of antibodies that attack the thyroid gland. These antibodies can cross the placenta and cause thyroid dysfunction in the fetus, leading to poor weight gain.",
