                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient in the scenario is a 25-year-old male who has sustained a penetrating wound to the chest during a knife fight. He is unconscious and has a low blood pressure, high heart rate, and low oxygen saturation. Physical examination reveals a profusely bleeding wound in the left side of the chest, which has caused a full thickness penetrating injury to the right ventricular free wall. The patient is intubated and aggressive fluid resuscitation is initiated, including a blood transfusion. The question asks for the best definitive surgical approach to take in this patient.\n\nOption A, immediate cardiac transplant, is not a feasible option in this scenario. Cardiac transplantation is a complex and time-consuming procedure that requires a suitable donor heart, a specialized surgical team, and a stable patient. It is not a practical option for a patient who is unstable and bleeding profusely.\n\nOption B, watchful waiting while resuscitative fluids are initiated, is not a definitive surgical approach. It is a supportive measure that aims to stabilize the patient's vital signs and improve his overall condition. However, it does not address the underlying problem of the penetrating injury to the right ventricular free wall.\n\nOption D, needle thoracostomy over the 2nd intercostal space, is a procedure that involves inserting a needle into the pleural space to relieve tension pneumothorax. It is not indicated in this scenario, as there is no evidence of pneumothorax. Moreover, the wound is in the left side of the chest, while the needle thoracostomy is performed on the right side.\n\nOption E, surgical adhesive glue, is not a definitive surgical approach for a full thickness penetrating injury to the right ventricular free wall. It may be used as an adjunct to other surgical techniques, but it cannot replace them.\n\nOption C, interrupted 2-0 polypropylene suture with supporting pledgets, is the best definitive surgical approach to take in this patient. This technique involves using a suture to close the wound in the right ventricular free wall, with the support of pledgets to reinforce the suture and prevent bleeding. It is a well-established and effective technique for repairing cardiac injuries, and it can be performed relatively quickly and safely in a stable patient. Therefore, option C is the correct answer.",
