                    {
                        "id": "3b66e129-720a-4027-9955-94c565457af7",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-16",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: A penetrating wound to the chest can cause significant damage to vital organs, including the heart and lungs. It is important to stabilize the patient's vital signs and address any bleeding before determining the best surgical approach.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the patient's current condition?\nThe patient is unconscious and has a penetrating wound to the chest that is bleeding profusely. Vitals show a low blood pressure, high heart rate, and low oxygen saturation.\n\n2. What is the extent of the injury?\nTransthoracic echocardiography reveals a full thickness penetrating injury to the right ventricular free wall. There are no apparent injuries to any coronary arteries or major branches.\n\n3. What is the goal of treatment?\nThe goal of treatment is to stabilize the patient's vital signs and address any bleeding before determining the best surgical approach.\n\n4. What are the available surgical approaches?\nThe available surgical approaches are immediate cardiac transplant, watchful waiting while resuscitative fluids are initiated, interrupted 2-0 polypropylene suture with supporting pledgets, needle thoracostomy over the 2nd intercostal space, and surgical adhesive glue.\n\nAnswer: \nBased on the patient's condition and the extent of the injury, the best definitive surgical approach to take in this patient is interrupted 2-0 polypropylene suture with supporting pledgets (option C). This approach involves suturing the wound closed with a strong, non-absorbable suture and using pledgets to reinforce the suture line. This will help to stop the bleeding and prevent further damage to the heart. Immediate cardiac transplant (option A) is not feasible in this situation due to the patient's unstable condition and the time required to find a suitable donor heart. Watchful waiting (option B) is not appropriate given the severity of the patient's condition. Needle thoracostomy (option D) is used to treat tension pneumothorax, which is not present in this case. Surgical adhesive glue (option E) is not appropriate for a full thickness penetrating injury to the heart.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "f28e5809-5b71-42b4-a96a-0bcaaf986852",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "C) Interrupted 2-0 polypropylene suture with supporting pledgets.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "C",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
