                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nDifficult. The question asks for the best definitive surgical approach to take in a 25-year-old male with a penetrating wound to the chest that has resulted in a full thickness penetrating injury to the right ventricular free wall. The patient is unconscious and has a low blood pressure, high heart rate, and low oxygen saturation. The options are:\n\nA) Immediate cardiac transplant\nB) Watchful waiting while resuscitative fluids are initiated\nC) Interrupted 2-0 polypropylene suture with supporting pledgets\nD) Needle thoracostomy over the 2nd intercostal space\nE) Surgical adhesive glue\n\nOption A is unlikely to be feasible or appropriate in this scenario due to the urgency of the situation and the need for immediate intervention. Option B may not be sufficient to address the patient's condition and could lead to further deterioration. Option E is unlikely to be effective in sealing a full thickness penetrating injury to the heart. \n\nOption D is a potential intervention for a tension pneumothorax, but it is not appropriate for a penetrating injury to the heart. \n\nOption C is the best choice for this scenario. Interrupted 2-0 polypropylene suture with supporting pledgets is a common surgical technique for repairing penetrating injuries to the heart. This approach can help to stop the bleeding and stabilize the patient's condition. However, it is important to note that this is a complex and high-risk procedure that should only be performed by experienced cardiac surgeons.",
