                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Identify the risk factors for melanoma.\nThe risk factors for melanoma include fair skin, history of sunburns, family history of melanoma, history of atypical moles, weakened immune system, and exposure to UV radiation.\n\nStep 2: Evaluate the patient's risk factors.\nThe patient is African-American, which means she has a lower risk of melanoma compared to individuals with fair skin. However, she has a family history of melanoma, which increases her risk. She also travels to the Carribean regularly, which increases her exposure to UV radiation.\n\nStep 3: Identify the types of melanoma.\nThere are four main types of melanoma: superficial spreading, nodular, lentigo maligna, and acral lentiginous. Desmoplastic melanoma is a rare subtype of melanoma.\n\nStep 4: Determine the type of melanoma that the patient is at greatest risk for.\nBased on the patient's risk factors, she is at greatest risk for acral lentiginous melanoma. This type of melanoma is more common in individuals with darker skin and is often found on the palms of the hands, soles of the feet, and under the nails. It is also associated with chronic sun exposure.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C) Acral lentiginous.",
