                        "cot_trigger": "zhou-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Identify the key information in the question.\n- 67-year-old man with peripheral neuropathy\n- Serum protein electrophoresis showed monoclonal gammopathy\n- Complete blood count, serum creatinine, and serum electrolyte concentrations are normal\n- Bone marrow biopsy shows 6% monoclonal plasma cells\n- Class I major histocompatibility molecules are downregulated in these monoclonal plasma cells\n- Proliferation of these monoclonal plasma cells is normally prevented by a class of immune cells that lyse abnormal cells without the need for opsonization, priming, or prior activation\n\nStep 2: Recall the function of different immune cells.\n- CD8+ T lymphocytes: cytotoxic T cells that recognize and kill infected or abnormal cells\n- Bone marrow-derived macrophages: phagocytic cells that engulf and digest foreign substances and cellular debris\n- CD4+ T lymphocytes: helper T cells that activate and coordinate other immune cells\n- Natural killer cells: innate immune cells that recognize and kill infected or abnormal cells without prior activation or priming\n- Band neutrophils: immature neutrophils that are released from the bone marrow during infection or inflammation\n\nStep 3: Determine the best answer.\n- The question asks for the class of immune cells that can lyse abnormal cells without the need for opsonization, priming, or prior activation.\n- The best answer is natural killer cells, as they are innate immune cells that can recognize and kill infected or abnormal cells without prior activation or priming.\n- CD8+ T lymphocytes can also recognize and kill abnormal cells, but they require activation and priming by antigen-presenting cells.\n- Bone marrow-derived macrophages, CD4+ T lymphocytes, and band neutrophils are not typically involved in the direct lysis of abnormal cells.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D) Natural killer cells.",
                        "answers": [
