                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient's symptoms and laboratory findings suggest a neuromuscular disorder. The weakness in the arm abductors and hip flexors bilaterally, along with elevated creatine kinase levels, suggest a myopathy. The difficulty with swallowing solid foods suggests involvement of the muscles of the pharynx and upper esophagus.\n\nOption A, intrafascicular infiltration on muscle biopsy, is seen in some types of myopathies, but is not specific enough to confirm a diagnosis.\n\nOption B, inflammation of synovial spaces on ultrasound, is not relevant to this patient's symptoms and laboratory findings.\n\nOption C, perifascicular and perivascular infiltration on muscle biopsy, is a characteristic finding in dermatomyositis, but this patient's symptoms and laboratory findings are not consistent with that diagnosis.\n\nOption D, positive anti-acetylcholine receptor antibodies, is seen in myasthenia gravis, which can cause muscle weakness and difficulty with swallowing. However, this patient's laboratory findings do not suggest myasthenia gravis, and the presence of a dystrophin gene mutation would be a more likely explanation for her symptoms.\n\nOption E, dystrophin gene mutation on genetic analysis, is the most likely answer. The patient's family history of Duchenne's muscular dystrophy, along with her symptoms and laboratory findings, suggest a dystrophinopathy. Genetic testing can confirm the diagnosis and identify the specific type of dystrophinopathy.",
