                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "First, based on the patient's symptoms and family history, we can suspect a possible muscular dystrophy. The elevated creatine kinase level also supports this suspicion. \n\nTo confirm the diagnosis, we need to perform further tests. Option A, intrafascicular infiltration on muscle biopsy, is a possible finding in some types of muscular dystrophy, but it is not specific enough to confirm the diagnosis. \n\nOption B, inflammation of synovial spaces on ultrasound, is not relevant to the diagnosis of muscular dystrophy. \n\nOption C, perifascicular and perivascular infiltration on muscle biopsy, is a characteristic finding in dermatomyositis, which is a type of inflammatory myopathy. However, the patient's symptoms and family history do not suggest dermatomyositis as the most likely diagnosis. \n\nOption D, positive anti-acetylcholine receptor antibodies, is a finding in myasthenia gravis, which is a neuromuscular disorder that can cause muscle weakness. However, the patient's symptoms and family history do not suggest myasthenia gravis as the most likely diagnosis. \n\nOption E, dystrophin gene mutation on genetic analysis, is the most likely test to confirm the diagnosis of muscular dystrophy. Duchenne's muscular dystrophy, which the patient's maternal uncle had, is caused by a mutation in the dystrophin gene. Genetic analysis can identify mutations in this gene and other genes associated with muscular dystrophy. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is E) Dystrophin gene mutation on genetic analysis.",
