                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Hi professor, can you explain the case to me again?\n\nProfessor: Sure, a 47-year-old woman is experiencing progressive muscle weakness for five months. She has difficulty getting up from chairs, climbing stairs, and combing her hair. She also has difficulty swallowing solid foods but not liquids. She has a history of hyperlipidemia controlled with fluvastatin. Her maternal uncle died from Duchenne's muscular dystrophy, and her mother has Hashimoto's thyroiditis. Neurologic examination shows moderate weakness in the arm abductors and hip flexors bilaterally. Laboratory studies show elevated creatine kinase and erythrocyte sedimentation rate.\n\nStudent: What are the options for confirming the diagnosis?\n\nProfessor: The options are intrafascicular infiltration on muscle biopsy, inflammation of synovial spaces on ultrasound, perifascicular and perivascular infiltration on muscle biopsy, positive anti-acetylcholine receptor antibodies, and dystrophin gene mutation on genetic analysis.\n\nStudent: Can you explain what each option means?\n\nProfessor: Intrafascicular infiltration on muscle biopsy means that there is inflammation within the fascicles of the muscle. Inflammation of synovial spaces on ultrasound means that there is inflammation within the joint spaces. Perifascicular and perivascular infiltration on muscle biopsy means that there is inflammation around the fascicles and blood vessels of the muscle. Positive anti-acetylcholine receptor antibodies means that the body is attacking the receptors that allow muscles to contract. Dystrophin gene mutation on genetic analysis means that there is a mutation in the gene that codes for dystrophin, a protein that helps maintain muscle structure.\n\nStudent: Which option is most likely to confirm the diagnosis?\n\nProfessor: The most likely option to confirm the diagnosis is dystrophin gene mutation on genetic analysis. Given the patient's family history and symptoms, it is likely that she has a form of muscular dystrophy, and a dystrophin gene mutation would confirm this.",
