                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "the patient's presentation and laboratory results suggest a diagnosis of Cushing's syndrome, likely caused by an adrenal adenoma or carcinoma. The next step in management would be to localize the source of excess cortisol production. Option A, an MRI of the adrenal glands, would be the most appropriate next step. This can help identify the presence of an adrenal mass and determine if it is benign or malignant. Option B, an MRI of the chest, would be indicated if there was suspicion of a lung tumor causing ectopic ACTH production. Option C, an MRI of the pituitary gland, would be indicated if there was suspicion of Cushing's disease caused by a pituitary adenoma. Option D, a low dose dexamethasone suppression test, would not be helpful in this case as the patient has already failed a high dose suppression test. Option E, inferior petrosal sinus sampling, would be indicated if there was suspicion of Cushing's disease and the MRI of the pituitary gland was inconclusive.",
                        "answers": [
