                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient's laboratory workup and clinical presentation are consistent with Cushing's syndrome, which is caused by excess cortisol production. The high 24-hour urinary cortisol and serum cortisol levels, as well as the lack of suppression with a high dose dexamethasone suppression test, suggest that the source of excess cortisol is likely adrenal in origin (as opposed to pituitary or ectopic). Therefore, the best next step in management would be to perform an MRI of the adrenal glands (option A) to look for any adrenal tumors or nodules that may be causing the excess cortisol production. Options B and C (MRI of the chest or pituitary gland) are less likely to be helpful in this case, as the patient's ACTH levels are low and there is no evidence of a pituitary adenoma on imaging. Option D (low dose dexamethasone suppression test) is not necessary, as the high dose test has already confirmed the diagnosis. Option E (inferior petrosal sinus sampling) may be considered if the MRI is inconclusive or if there is suspicion of an ectopic source of ACTH, but it is not the best initial step in management. Therefore, the correct answer is A) MRI of the adrenal glands.",
                        "answers": [
