                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe presence of an intrauterine heteroechoic mass with numerous anechoic spaces and no identifiable fetus or amniotic fluid suggests a molar pregnancy or a hydatidiform mole. This is a rare condition where the fertilized egg develops into a tumor instead of a fetus. The enlarged ovaries with multiple thin-walled, septated cysts with clear content are suggestive of ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome (OHSS), which can occur in molar pregnancies due to the high levels of human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) hormone produced by the tumor.\n\nAmong the given options, the most likely cause of the ovarian findings in this case is E) Theca lutein cysts, which are a type of functional ovarian cysts that can occur in response to high levels of hCG hormone. Theca lutein cysts are typically bilateral and can be large and numerous, with thin walls and clear fluid content. They are commonly associated with molar pregnancies, multiple gestations, and fertility treatments that involve hCG injections.\n\nA) Chocolate cysts are a type of endometriotic cysts that contain old blood and debris, and are typically associated with endometriosis.\n\nB) Corpus luteum cysts are functional cysts that form from the corpus luteum after ovulation, and can produce progesterone. They are usually small and resolve on their own.\n\nC) Yolk sac tumor is a rare type of ovarian germ cell tumor that can produce hCG and alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) hormones. It is typically solid and has a characteristic \"fried-egg\" appearance on imaging.\n\nD) Luteoma of pregnancy is a rare benign tumor of the ovary that can occur during pregnancy and produce androgens and estrogens. It is typically solid and can cause virilization or feminization of the fetus.",
                        "answers": [
