                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient is a 70-year-old woman with a history of hypertension and gastroesophageal reflux who presents with burning pain in her upper right back and chest, along with a long erythematous rash covered with multiple clear vesicles and crusty lesions extending from her back to below her right breast. She has no recent injury and no prior history of back pain. She does not smoke or drink alcohol and takes ramipril and pantoprazole. She volunteers at an animal shelter three times a week. Her vital signs are within normal limits, except for her blood pressure, which is elevated.\n\nThe presence of a long erythematous rash covered with multiple clear vesicles and crusty lesions extending from her back to below her right breast suggests a dermatological condition. The fact that the rash is unilateral and follows a dermatomal distribution suggests that it may be related to a nerve root. The patient's history of hypertension and gastroesophageal reflux is not directly relevant to the diagnosis of her current condition.\n\nOption A, viral reactivation in dorsal root ganglia, is a possible mechanism of the disease. Viral infections such as herpes zoster (shingles) can cause a painful rash in a dermatomal distribution. The patient's age and history of volunteering at an animal shelter may increase her risk of exposure to viral infections. However, there is no mention of prodromal symptoms such as fever or malaise, which are common in viral infections.\n\nOption B, IgA deposits in the upper dermis, is not a likely mechanism of the disease. IgA deposits are associated with autoimmune diseases such as dermatitis herpetiformis, which typically presents with a symmetrical rash on the extensor surfaces of the limbs and trunk. The patient's rash is unilateral and follows a dermatomal distribution.\n\nOption C, bacterial lymphatic infection of the epidermis, is not a likely mechanism of the disease. Bacterial infections such as cellulitis can cause a rash, but they typically present with diffuse erythema and swelling, not a dermatomal distribution. The presence of clear vesicles and crusty lesions is also not typical of bacterial infections.\n\nOption D, infestation with bloodsucking parasites, is not a likely mechanism of the disease. Parasitic infestations such as scabies can cause a rash, but they typically present with diffuse erythema and papules, not a dermatomal distribution. The presence of clear vesicles and crusty lesions is also not typical of parasitic infestations.\n\nOption E",
                        "answers": [
