                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient is a 35-year-old Caucasian female with unilateral leg swelling and redness. She has a history of a thrombus in the right femoral vein one year ago. On examination, the left leg is erythematous and swollen, and passive dorsiflexion of the left ankle elicits pain in the left calf. Ultrasound of the leg reveals a thrombus in the left popliteal vein. A genetic workup reveals that she has an inherited condition. \n\nBased on the patient's history and examination findings, the most likely diagnosis is deep vein thrombosis (DVT). The fact that she has a history of a thrombus in the right femoral vein and now has a thrombus in the left popliteal vein suggests that she may have an underlying genetic condition that predisposes her to DVT. \n\nThe answer choices are:\n\nA) Elevated serum homocysteine\nB) Thrombin inhibitor deficiency\nC) Deficiency in protein C\nD) Auto-antibodies directed against phospholipids\nE) Resistance to clotting factor degradation\n\nElevated serum homocysteine is a risk factor for DVT, but it is not an inherited condition. Thrombin inhibitor deficiency and deficiency in protein C are both inherited conditions that can increase the risk of DVT, but they typically present earlier in life and are associated with a higher risk of recurrent DVT. Auto-antibodies directed against phospholipids are associated with antiphospholipid syndrome, which can cause DVT, but it typically presents with other symptoms such as recurrent miscarriages and thrombocytopenia. \n\nResistance to clotting factor degradation is a genetic condition known as factor V Leiden mutation, which is the most common inherited risk factor for DVT. It is associated with a higher risk of DVT in young individuals and a higher risk of recurrent DVT. Therefore, the most likely pathophysiology of this patient's condition is resistance to clotting factor degradation, or factor V Leiden mutation. \n\nThe correct answer is E) Resistance to clotting factor degradation.",
                        "answers": [
