                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The question is asking what will happen when a mischarged Lys-tRNA with valine is inserted into a cell and used in protein translation with an mRNA sequence that only contains codons for lysine. \n\nOption A: The ribosome will recognize the mischarged tRNA and prevent its incorporation by removing the valine. This is unlikely as the ribosome does not have proofreading mechanisms to remove mischarged tRNAs. \n\nOption B: The ribosome will recognize the inappropriately inserted valine residue and terminate protein translation. This is also unlikely as the ribosome does not have the ability to recognize individual amino acids in the growing peptide chain. \n\nOption C: The mischarged tRNA with valine will be incorporated in the codons that specify for lysine. This is the most likely scenario as the ribosome will read the lysine codons and incorporate the available tRNA, which happens to be the mischarged Lys-tRNA with valine. \n\nOption D: The mischarged tRNA will be degraded by the proteasome. This is unlikely as the proteasome is involved in degrading proteins, not tRNAs. \n\nOption E: The mischarged tRNA will be removed by the lysyl-tRNA synthetase. This is also unlikely as the lysyl-tRNA synthetase is responsible for charging tRNAs with lysine, not removing mischarged tRNAs. \n\nTherefore, the most likely scenario is that the mischarged tRNA with valine will be incorporated in the codons that specify for lysine. The resulting protein will have valine instead of lysine at the corresponding positions in the peptide chain.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "22cfeabb-6d25-4d52-82c8-e3ab3fdf4434",
