                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Rephrased question: What is the most likely outcome when a laboratory charges a Lys-tRNA with valine and inserts it into a cell with an mRNA sequence containing only codons for lysine, in order to investigate whether the ribosome can recognize a mischarged amino acid and still incorporate it into the growing peptide?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n- The laboratory is investigating whether the ribosome can recognize a mischarged amino acid and still incorporate it into the growing peptide.\n- They charge a Lys-tRNA with valine instead of lysine and insert it into the cell.\n- They design an mRNA sequence that contains only codons for lysine.\n- The ribosome will read the mRNA sequence and recruit the appropriate tRNA to add the corresponding amino acid to the growing peptide chain.\n- In this case, the ribosome will encounter a mischarged tRNA with valine instead of lysine.\n- Option A suggests that the ribosome will recognize the mischarged tRNA and prevent its incorporation by removing the valine. However, there is no known mechanism for the ribosome to remove an amino acid from a tRNA.\n- Option B suggests that the ribosome will recognize the inappropriately inserted valine residue and terminate protein translation. This is a possible outcome, as the ribosome has a quality control mechanism that can detect errors and stall translation.\n- Option C suggests that the mischarged tRNA with valine will be incorporated in the codons that specify for lysine. This is also a possible outcome, as the ribosome may not be able to distinguish between lysine and valine in this context.\n- Option D suggests that the mischarged tRNA will be degraded by the proteasome. However, the proteasome is not involved in tRNA degradation.\n- Option E suggests that the mischarged tRNA will be removed by the lysyl-tRNA synthetase. However, lysyl-tRNA synthetase is not involved in tRNA removal.\n\nCorrect answer: B) The ribosome will recognize the inappropriately inserted valine residue and terminate protein translation.",
