                        "cot_trigger_template": "",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Let's derive the differential diagnosis step by step: \n\nThe small pelvis, shallow intergluteal cleft, and club feet are all features of congenital diaphragmatic hernia (CDH). CDH is a birth defect in which the diaphragm doesn't form properly, and the intestines and other abdominal organs move into the chest cavity. This can cause life-threatening respiratory problems.\n\nThe absence of the sacrum and lumbar vertebrae is a feature of caudal regression syndrome (CRS). CRS is a rare congenital disorder characterized by the partial or complete absence of the sacrum and lumbar vertebrae. This can lead to problems with the urinary and digestive systems, and can cause paralysis of the lower limbs.\n\nThe strongest predisposing factor for CDH is intrauterine alcohol exposure. The strongest predisposing factor for CRS is chromosome 18 trisomy.",
