                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "What is the most likely initial metastatic site in a 48-year-old female with a thyroid nodule suspicious for neoplasia?\n\nStep-by-step:\n\n1. The patient presented with a lump in her neck and was found to have a fixed, immobile, and non-tender thyroid nodule on examination.\n2. Ultrasound showed a hypoechoic nodule with a size of 2 cm.\n3. A fine needle biopsy was performed and cytological examination reported a likely suspicion of neoplasia.\n4. CT scan was performed to check for any lesions in the bones and/or lungs, common metastatic sites in this condition.\n5. Treatment with radioiodine therapy is planned after near-total thyroidectomy.\n6. The question is asking for the most likely initial metastatic site in this patient.\n\nAnswer: C) Cervical lymph nodes\n\nReasoning: The most common initial site of metastasis for thyroid cancer is the cervical lymph nodes, which are located in the neck where the thyroid gland is located. The fact that the patient presented with a lump in her neck and had a thyroid nodule that was suspicious for neoplasia on biopsy suggests that the cancer has already spread to the nearby lymph nodes. The CT scan was performed to check for metastasis to other sites, but the question is asking for the most likely initial site of metastasis. Therefore, the answer is cervical lymph nodes.",
