                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Hi professor, I have a question about the case study. What could be the cause of the patient's pelvic pain?\n\nProfessor: Good question. The most likely cause of the patient's symptoms is endometrial sloughing and uterine contractions mediated by prostaglandin.\n\nStudent: How does endometrial sloughing cause pelvic pain?\n\nProfessor: Endometrial sloughing is the shedding of the lining of the uterus, which occurs during menstruation. This shedding is caused by the release of prostaglandins, which cause the uterus to contract and expel the lining. These contractions can cause pain, especially if they are strong or prolonged.\n\nStudent: Why does the pain radiate to her back and thighs?\n\nProfessor: The pain can radiate to the back and thighs because the uterus and surrounding muscles are all connected by nerves. When the uterus contracts, it can stimulate these nerves and cause pain in other areas.\n\nStudent: Why does the pain occur a few hours before her menstrual period and last for 2 days?\n\nProfessor: The pain occurs a few hours before her menstrual period because that is when the prostaglandin levels are highest. The pain lasts for 2 days because that is how long it takes for the uterus to expel the lining.\n\nStudent: Could any other conditions cause these symptoms?\n\nProfessor: Yes, there are other conditions that could cause similar symptoms, such as an ascending infection of the uterus, fallopian tubes, ovaries, or surrounding tissue, a fluid-filled sac within the ovary, a hormone-sensitive smooth muscle tumor of the myometrium, or even pregnancy. However, based on the information provided, endometrial sloughing is the most likely cause.",
