                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: The Beta-globin gene is responsible for producing a protein called beta-globin, which is a component of hemoglobin. Hemoglobin is the protein in red blood cells that carries oxygen throughout the body. Thalassemia intermedia is a type of thalassemia, which is a group of inherited blood disorders that affect the production of hemoglobin.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the Kozak sequence?\n2. What is the mutation in the Kozak sequence of the Beta-globin gene?\n3. How does the mutation affect translation of the Beta-globin mRNA?\n4. What is thalassemia intermedia?\n5. How does thalassemia intermedia affect red blood cells?\n6. What would the blood smear show in a patient positive for the mutation?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The Kozak sequence is a sequence of nucleotides that is found in the mRNA of eukaryotic genes. It is important for the initiation of translation, which is the process by which the mRNA is used to produce a protein.\n2. The mutation in the Kozak sequence of the Beta-globin gene is a single nucleotide change from G to A at position +4 relative to the start codon.\n3. The mutation decreases the efficiency of translation of the Beta-globin mRNA, but does not completely abolish it.\n4. Thalassemia intermedia is a type of thalassemia that is less severe than thalassemia major, but more severe than thalassemia minor. It is caused by mutations in the genes that produce hemoglobin.\n5. Thalassemia intermedia can cause a variety of symptoms, including anemia, fatigue, jaundice, and bone deformities. It can also affect the size and shape of red blood cells.\n6. The blood smear in a patient positive for the mutation would be expected to show microcytic red blood cells, which are smaller than normal, and hypochromic red blood cells, which have less hemoglobin than normal. Bite cells, which are red blood cells that have been damaged by the spleen, may also be present. Ringed sideroblasts, which are red blood cells with iron granules around the nucleus, are not typically seen in thalassemia intermedia. Therefore, the answer is C) Microcytic red blood cells.",
