                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Plan:\n1. Identify the patient's symptoms and medical history.\n2. Review the physical examination findings and diagnostic tests.\n3. Determine the acid-base status of the patient based on the ABG results.\n4. Choose the answer option that best matches the ABG findings.\n\nAnswer: B) Primary respiratory acidosis\n\nExplanation:\nThe patient presents with a chronic cough and shortness of breath, which have worsened over the last 2 days. He also reports body aches, headache, and fever, suggesting an acute exacerbation of his chronic respiratory condition. The physical examination reveals diminished breath sounds bilateral, a barrel-shaped chest, and measured breathing through pursed lips, which are consistent with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). The chest X-ray shows a flattened diaphragm, which is a characteristic finding in patients with COPD. The pulmonary function testing reveals an FEV1/FVC ratio of 60%, which is consistent with obstructive lung disease. The ABG results are most likely to reveal primary respiratory acidosis, which is a common complication of COPD. The patient's respiratory rate is normal, but his breathing is measured through pursed lips, which suggests that he is trying to compensate for his respiratory acidosis by increasing his expiratory time and reducing his respiratory rate. The other answer options are less likely based on the patient's clinical presentation and diagnostic tests.",
