                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Identify the patient's symptoms and physical exam findings.\nThe patient presents with excessive fatigue and weakness, abdominal pain, constipation, and weight loss. He also has a bronze discoloration to his skin.\n\nStep 2: Consider the differential diagnosis.\nThe combination of symptoms and physical exam findings suggests a possible adrenal insufficiency, also known as Addison's disease. Other possible causes of fatigue, weakness, and weight loss include depression, malignancy, and chronic infections.\n\nStep 3: Determine the next best step in management.\nThe next best step in management is to confirm the diagnosis of adrenal insufficiency. This can be done by ordering an ACTH stimulation test, which measures the adrenal gland's ability to produce cortisol in response to ACTH stimulation. If the test confirms adrenal insufficiency, the patient should be started on hydrocortisone therapy.\n\nStep 4: Evaluate the answer choices.\nA) Administer intravenous hydrocortisone - This may be necessary in an acute adrenal crisis, but it is not the next best step in management for this patient.\nB) Administer intravenous fludrocortisone - Fludrocortisone is used to replace aldosterone in patients with primary adrenal insufficiency, but it is not indicated in this patient with suspected secondary adrenal insufficiency.\nC) Start him on androgen replacement treatment - Androgen replacement is not indicated in this patient with suspected adrenal insufficiency.\nD) Start him on both hydrocortisone and fludrocortisone therapy - Fludrocortisone is not indicated in this patient with suspected secondary adrenal insufficiency.\nE) Order an ACTH stimulation test - This is the correct answer as it is the next best step in management to confirm the diagnosis of adrenal insufficiency.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is E) Order an ACTH stimulation test.",
