                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "it is important to note that ELISA, Western blot, and rapid HIV antibody tests are used to detect antibodies produced by the body in response to HIV infection, not the virus itself. Therefore, these tests would not be useful in detecting HIV infection in a newborn.\n\nThe correct answer is D) Polymerase chain reaction (PCR). PCR is a sensitive and specific test that can detect the genetic material (RNA) of HIV in a blood sample. It is commonly used to diagnose HIV infection in infants born to HIV-positive mothers, as infants may not produce detectable levels of HIV antibodies for several months after birth. PCR can detect HIV infection as early as 2 weeks after birth.",
