                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient presents with a cord-like rash on her left calf that is red and painful to touch, which she has had multiple times before. She also has a lacy mottled violaceous rash on multiple extremities. These symptoms suggest a diagnosis of dermatomyositis, an autoimmune disease that affects the skin and muscles. \n\nThe patient's medical history reveals that she has had 3 past spontaneous abortions at < 10 weeks of gestational age, which may be related to an underlying autoimmune condition. \n\nGiven the diagnosis of dermatomyositis, the most likely antibody to be found in this patient's blood is anti-ribonucleoprotein (anti-RNP). Anti-RNP antibodies are commonly found in patients with dermatomyositis, as well as in patients with mixed connective tissue disease (MCTD), which shares some clinical features with dermatomyositis. \n\nThe other antibodies listed (anti-cardiolipin, anti-centromere, anti-helicase, and anti-histone) are associated with other autoimmune diseases, such as antiphospholipid syndrome, systemic sclerosis, systemic lupus erythematosus, and drug-induced lupus, respectively. These conditions are less likely to be the cause of the patient's symptoms based on the information provided.",
