                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: The patient has a cord-like rash on her left calf that is red and painful to touch, which she has had multiple times before. She also has a lacy mottled violaceous rash on multiple extremities. She has a history of spontaneous abortions and has never been diagnosed with any diseases. She drinks socially but has never smoked or used drugs. She has never taken any medications except for over the counter analgesics and antipyretics.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the most likely diagnosis for the patient's cord-like rash and lacy mottled violaceous rash?\n2. What antibodies are associated with the most likely diagnosis?\n3. Which of the listed antibodies is most likely to be found in the patient's blood?\n\nAnswer:\n1. The patient's symptoms are consistent with dermatomyositis, an autoimmune disease that affects the skin and muscles. The cord-like rash is called a Gottron's papule and is a characteristic feature of dermatomyositis. The lacy mottled violaceous rash is also a common skin manifestation of the disease.\n2. Dermatomyositis is associated with several autoantibodies, including anti-Mi-2, anti-Jo-1, anti-SRP, and anti-PL-7. Anti-Mi-2 is the most common autoantibody associated with dermatomyositis and is found in up to 25% of patients.\n3. None of the listed antibodies are specifically associated with dermatomyositis. However, anti-ribonucleoprotein (anti-RNP) is commonly found in patients with mixed connective tissue disease, which shares some clinical features with dermatomyositis. Therefore, E) Anti-ribonucleoprotein is the most likely antibody to be found in this patient's blood.",
