                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Identify the key features of the patient's presentation.\n- Cord-like rash on left calf that is red and painful to touch\n- Multiple previous similar lesions\n- History of 3 spontaneous abortions at < 10 weeks of gestational age\n- No previous diagnosis of any diseases\n- Social drinker, non-smoker, no drug use\n- No medication use except for over the counter analgesics and antipyretics\n- Lacy mottled violaceous rash on multiple extremities\n\nStep 2: Consider the possible diagnoses based on the patient's presentation.\n- The cord-like rash on the left calf that is red and painful to touch suggests a possible diagnosis of erythema nodosum.\n- The history of multiple similar lesions and the lacy mottled violaceous rash on multiple extremities suggest a possible diagnosis of cutaneous vasculitis.\n- The history of 3 spontaneous abortions at < 10 weeks of gestational age raises the possibility of an underlying autoimmune disorder.\n\nStep 3: Consider the possible antibodies that may be found in autoimmune disorders associated with cutaneous vasculitis and spontaneous abortions.\n- Anti-cardiolipin antibodies are associated with antiphospholipid syndrome, which can cause cutaneous vasculitis and spontaneous abortions.\n- Anti-centromere antibodies are associated with limited systemic sclerosis, which can cause cutaneous vasculitis but is not typically associated with spontaneous abortions.\n- Anti-helicase antibodies are not typically associated with cutaneous vasculitis or spontaneous abortions.\n- Anti-histone antibodies are associated with drug-induced lupus, which can cause cutaneous vasculitis but is not typically associated with spontaneous abortions.\n- Anti-ribonucleoprotein antibodies are associated with mixed connective tissue disease, which can cause cutaneous vasculitis and spontaneous abortions.\n\nStep 4: Choose the most likely antibody based on the patient's presentation.\n- Given the patient's history of cutaneous vasculitis and spontaneous abortions, the most likely antibody to be found in her blood is anti-ribonucleoprotein (anti-RNP) antibodies, which are associated with mixed connective tissue disease.\n\nAnswer: E) Anti-ribonucleoprotein",
