                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what an irregular gait means?\n\nProfessor: An irregular gait means that the way the child walks is not normal or smooth. It could be due to a problem with the muscles, bones, or nerves in the legs.\n\nStudent: What does it mean when the left leg drops and the pelvis tilts towards the left?\n\nProfessor: When the left leg drops, it means that the child is not able to support his weight on that leg properly. The pelvis tilting towards the left is a compensatory mechanism to maintain balance.\n\nStudent: Why did the physician examine the lower extremities?\n\nProfessor: The lower extremities include the legs and feet, which are the parts of the body that are responsible for walking and standing. Examining them can help the physician identify any problems that may be causing the irregular gait.\n\nStudent: Why is it important to check for redness or swelling?\n\nProfessor: Redness or swelling at the injection site could indicate an infection or an allergic reaction to the vaccine.\n\nStudent: So, which location is the most likely cause of the symptoms?\n\nProfessor: The symptoms are most likely due to the injection of the vaccine into the inferomedial quadrant of the left buttock (option C). This is because the left leg dropping and the pelvis tilting towards the left suggest weakness in the left gluteal muscles, which are located in the buttock. The inferomedial quadrant of the left buttock is where the vaccine would have been injected, and it is close to the location of the gluteal muscles.",
