                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The symptoms and physical examination findings suggest a possible diagnosis of pneumonia with oral thrush and lymphadenopathy. The infant's low weight and lack of immunizations also raise concerns for possible underlying immunodeficiency. Therefore, the most likely test to confirm the diagnosis would be a polymerase chain reaction (PCR) for viral genes, which can identify the specific pathogen causing the pneumonia. Option A (karyotyping) and option B (CFTR mutation test) are not relevant to the current presentation. Option C (fluorescent treponemal antibody absorption test) is used to diagnose syphilis, which is not a likely cause of the infant's symptoms. Option E (T-cell receptor excision circle analysis) is used to evaluate for T-cell deficiencies, but this is not the most likely diagnosis based on the current presentation. Therefore, the correct answer is D) Polymerase chain reaction for viral genes.",
