                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: The innate immune system is the body's first line of defense against infections. It includes physical barriers, such as the skin and mucous membranes, as well as cellular and molecular components that recognize and respond to pathogens. The complement system is a key component of the innate immune system, consisting of a group of proteins that work together to identify and eliminate pathogens.\n\nAdditional facts: The patient has a history of meningitis and infection with Streptococcus pneumoniae, which suggests a susceptibility to bacterial infections. The laboratory results indicate that the patient has a bacterial infection caused by diplococci, which are often associated with Neisseria species.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the complement system?\n2. What is opsonization and how does it relate to the complement system?\n3. What is angioedema and how does it relate to the complement system?\n4. What is C1 esterase inhibitor and how does it relate to the complement system?\n5. What is the membrane attack complex and how does it relate to the complement system?\n6. Which component of the complement system is likely deficient in this patient?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The complement system is a group of proteins that work together to identify and eliminate pathogens. It can be activated through three pathways: the classical pathway, the alternative pathway, and the lectin pathway.\n2. Opsonization is the process by which pathogens are marked for destruction by phagocytic cells. The complement system can act as an opsonin by coating pathogens with complement proteins that are recognized by phagocytic cells.\n3. Angioedema is a condition characterized by swelling of the skin and mucous membranes. It can be caused by activation of the complement system, specifically the C1 esterase inhibitor pathway.\n4. C1 esterase inhibitor is a protein that regulates the activation of the complement system. Deficiencies in C1 esterase inhibitor can lead to overactivation of the complement system and the development of angioedema.\n5. The membrane attack complex is a group of complement proteins that form pores in the cell membrane of pathogens, leading to their destruction.\n6. Based on the patient's history of recurrent bacterial infections and the laboratory results indicating a bacterial infection caused by diplococci, it is likely that the patient has a deficiency in the complement component that creates pores in the cell membrane (option D). This component is known as the membrane attack complex and is responsible for directly killing pathogens. Deficiencies in this component can lead to recurrent bacterial",
