                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Identify the patient's condition and symptoms.\nThe patient is a 52-year-old man who drinks 10-12 beers daily and has not seen a physician in 15 years. He is presenting with recurrent vomiting that began 5 hours ago, with vomitus that is bloody and contains black specks. He appears pale, has a high pulse rate, low blood pressure, decreased radial pulses, delayed capillary refill, multiple spider angiomata, and a distended abdomen. He is oriented to person but not to place or time.\n\nStep 2: Determine what is most likely decreased in this patient in response to his current condition compared to a healthy adult.\nThe question is asking us to identify what is most likely decreased in this patient compared to a healthy adult. We need to consider the patient's symptoms and condition to determine the answer.\n\nSystemic vascular resistance (A) is the resistance to blood flow offered by all of the systemic vasculature, excluding the pulmonary vasculature. It is regulated by the tone of the arterioles and precapillary sphincters. In this patient, we might expect systemic vascular resistance to be increased due to the body's compensatory response to low blood pressure. Therefore, A is unlikely to be the correct answer.\n\nFractional tissue oxygen extraction (B) is the ratio of oxygen consumption to oxygen delivery in the tissues. It reflects the ability of the tissues to extract oxygen from the blood. In this patient, we might expect fractional tissue oxygen extraction to be increased due to the body's compensatory response to low blood pressure and decreased oxygen delivery. Therefore, B is unlikely to be the correct answer.\n\nCarotid baroreceptor firing activity (C) is the activity of the baroreceptors located in the carotid sinus, which sense changes in blood pressure and send signals to the brain to regulate blood pressure. In this patient, we might expect carotid baroreceptor firing activity to be increased due to the body's compensatory response to low blood pressure. Therefore, C is unlikely to be the correct answer.\n\nPulmonary vascular resistance (D) is the resistance to blood flow offered by the pulmonary vasculature. It is regulated by the tone of the pulmonary arterioles. In this patient, we might expect pulmonary vascular resistance to be decreased due to the body's compensatory response to low blood pressure. Therefore, D is unlikely to be the correct answer.\n\nCardiac inotropy (E) is the force of contraction of the heart muscle",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "8a8064d2-87c2-4e88-b8f1-5d99ddd8c143",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "E) Cardiac inotropy. In this patient, we might expect cardiac inotropy to be decreased due to the body's compensatory response to low blood pressure and decreased oxygen delivery. This can lead to decreased cardiac output and further exacerbate the patient's condition.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "E",
