                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Sickle cell anemia is a genetic disorder that affects the hemoglobin molecule in red blood cells. The mutation results in the production of abnormal hemoglobin (HgbS) that can cause the red blood cells to become sickle-shaped and less flexible. This can lead to blockages in blood vessels and reduced oxygen delivery to tissues, causing pain and organ damage. Hydroxyurea is a medication that can increase the production of fetal hemoglobin (HgbF), which can help prevent sickling of red blood cells.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the effect of the HgbS mutation on red blood cells?\n2. What is hydroxyurea?\n3. How does hydroxyurea affect hemoglobin physiology?\n4. What is the role of fetal hemoglobin (HgbF) in sickle cell anemia?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The HgbS mutation causes red blood cells to become sickle-shaped and less flexible, which can lead to blockages in blood vessels and reduced oxygen delivery to tissues.\n2. Hydroxyurea is a medication that can increase the production of fetal hemoglobin (HgbF) in red blood cells.\n3. Hydroxyurea increases the production of HgbF, which can help prevent sickling of red blood cells by reducing the amount of HgbS in the cells.\n4. Fetal hemoglobin (HgbF) is a type of hemoglobin that is normally produced during fetal development and is gradually replaced by adult hemoglobin (HgbA) after birth. HgbF has a higher affinity for oxygen than HgbS, which can help prevent sickling of red blood cells. In patients with sickle cell anemia, increasing the production of HgbF can help reduce the amount of HgbS in the cells and prevent sickling.",
                        "answers": [
