                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what sickle cell anemia is and how it affects hemoglobin?\n\nProfessor: Sure. Sickle cell anemia is a genetic disorder that affects the shape of red blood cells. Normally, red blood cells are round and flexible, but in sickle cell anemia, they become rigid and take on a crescent or sickle shape. This can cause blockages in blood vessels, leading to pain, organ damage, and other complications. The mutation that causes sickle cell anemia also affects the type of hemoglobin that is produced, resulting in the production of HgbS.\n\nStudent: What is hydroxyurea and how does it help treat sickle cell anemia?\n\nProfessor: Hydroxyurea is a medication that is often used to treat sickle cell anemia. It works by increasing the production of fetal hemoglobin (HgbF), which is a type of hemoglobin that is normally only produced in the fetus. HgbF is able to prevent the sickling of red blood cells, so by increasing its levels in patients with sickle cell anemia, hydroxyurea can help reduce the frequency and severity of symptoms.\n\nStudent: So, does hydroxyurea increase or decrease the oxygen carrying capacity of hemoglobin?\n\nProfessor: Hydroxyurea does not directly affect the oxygen carrying capacity of hemoglobin. However, by increasing the levels of HgbF, it can indirectly improve oxygen delivery to tissues by preventing the sickling of red blood cells.\n\nStudent: And does hydroxyurea increase or decrease the levels of HgbS?\n\nProfessor: Hydroxyurea can actually decrease the levels of HgbS by promoting the production of HgbF, which can replace some of the HgbS in the patient's blood. This can help reduce the frequency and severity of sickle cell crises.",
