                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step 1: Identify the key symptoms and findings in the patient's presentation - eye dryness, foreign body sensation in the eyes, mucosal ulceration and atrophy in the oral cavity, and lymphocytic infiltration of the minor salivary glands.\n\nStep 2: Consider the possible diagnoses that could explain these symptoms and findings. One possibility is Sj\u00f6gren's syndrome, an autoimmune disorder that affects the moisture-producing glands in the body, including the salivary and lacrimal glands. Other autoimmune disorders, such as systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) and scleroderma, could also be considered.\n\nStep 3: Review the options provided in the question stem and eliminate those that are not associated with Sj\u00f6gren's syndrome or other autoimmune disorders that could explain the patient's symptoms. Anti-centromere antibody is associated with limited systemic sclerosis, while anti-Jo-1 antibody is associated with polymyositis and dermatomyositis. Anti-Scl-70 antibodies are associated with diffuse systemic sclerosis, but this disorder typically presents with skin thickening and fibrosis, which are not mentioned in the patient's presentation. Anti-double stranded (-ds)DNA is associated with SLE, but the patient's symptoms do not suggest this diagnosis.\n\nStep 4: Choose the answer that is most likely to be seen in a patient with Sj\u00f6gren's syndrome. Anti-Sj\u00f6gren's syndrome type B (SS-B) antibody is commonly found in patients with Sj\u00f6gren's syndrome, along with anti-SS-A antibodies. These antibodies are thought to play a role in the destruction of the salivary and lacrimal glands, leading to the dryness and other symptoms seen in this disorder.\n\nAnswer: A) Anti-Sj\u00f6gren's syndrome type B (SS-B) antibody",
