                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient has been diagnosed with invasive ductal adenocarcinoma that is estrogen receptor positive and is being treated with tamoxifen. Tamoxifen is a selective estrogen receptor modulator (SERM) that is used to treat breast cancer. However, it has been associated with several potential complications, including:\n\nA) Deep venous thrombosis: Tamoxifen has been associated with an increased risk of blood clots, including deep venous thrombosis (DVT) and pulmonary embolism (PE). Patients taking tamoxifen should be monitored for signs and symptoms of DVT/PE, such as swelling, pain, and redness in the affected limb.\n\nB) Endometrial cancer: Tamoxifen has been associated with an increased risk of endometrial cancer, particularly in postmenopausal women. Patients taking tamoxifen should be monitored for abnormal vaginal bleeding or discharge, which may be a sign of endometrial cancer.\n\nC) Eruption of seborrheic keratoses: Seborrheic keratoses are benign skin growths that are common in older adults. Tamoxifen has been associated with an increased risk of eruptive seborrheic keratoses, which may appear suddenly and in large numbers. While not harmful, these growths can be unsightly and may cause concern for patients.\n\nD) Increased bleeding: Tamoxifen can interfere with blood clotting and increase the risk of bleeding. Patients taking tamoxifen should be monitored for signs of bleeding, such as easy bruising, nosebleeds, or bleeding gums.\n\nE) Osteoporosis: Tamoxifen can interfere with bone metabolism and increase the risk of osteoporosis, particularly in postmenopausal women. Patients taking tamoxifen should be monitored for bone density and may require calcium and vitamin D supplementation or other treatments to prevent osteoporosis.\n\nOf these potential complications, the most likely in this patient is B) endometrial cancer, given her age and past medical history. However, all of these complications should be considered and monitored for in patients taking tamoxifen.",
                        "answers": [
