                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what invasive ductal adenocarcinoma is?\n\nProfessor: Invasive ductal adenocarcinoma is a type of breast cancer that starts in the milk ducts and then invades the surrounding breast tissue.\n\nStudent: What does it mean that the cancer is estrogen receptor positive?\n\nProfessor: Estrogen receptor positive means that the cancer cells have receptors for estrogen, which can stimulate their growth. This information is important because it can help guide treatment decisions.\n\nStudent: What is tamoxifen and why is the patient taking it?\n\nProfessor: Tamoxifen is a medication that blocks the effects of estrogen on breast tissue. It is often used in patients with estrogen receptor positive breast cancer to help prevent recurrence of the cancer.\n\nStudent: What is a uterine leimyoma?\n\nProfessor: A uterine leimyoma, also known as a uterine fibroid, is a noncancerous growth in the uterus. It can cause symptoms such as heavy menstrual bleeding, pelvic pain, and pressure.\n\nStudent: What is endometrial cancer?\n\nProfessor: Endometrial cancer is a type of cancer that starts in the lining of the uterus. It can cause symptoms such as abnormal vaginal bleeding and pelvic pain.\n\nStudent: Can you explain what deep venous thrombosis is?\n\nProfessor: Deep venous thrombosis is a blood clot that forms in a deep vein, usually in the leg. It can cause swelling, pain, and redness in the affected area. If the clot breaks off and travels to the lungs, it can cause a life-threatening condition called pulmonary embolism.\n\nStudent: Why might a patient with breast cancer be at risk for deep venous thrombosis?\n\nProfessor: Patients with cancer are at increased risk for developing blood clots, including deep venous thrombosis. This is thought to be due to a combination of factors, including the cancer itself, chemotherapy, and other treatments.\n\nStudent: What is osteoporosis?\n\nProfessor: Osteoporosis is a condition in which the bones become weak and brittle, increasing the risk of fractures. It is more common in women, especially after menopause, and can be caused by a variety of factors including hormonal changes, certain medications, and lifestyle factors such as lack of exercise and poor nutrition.\n\nStudent: Why might a patient with breast cancer be at risk for osteoporosis?\n\nProfessor: Patients with breast cancer who are taking certain medications, such as aromatase inhibitors, may be at increased risk for osteopor",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "f0ba9d7a-dcaa-474b-b9ba-940ff32e4413",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
